// Phase 08 Task 1 -- Pattern A (ReturnsBorrowOfParam), REFUSING member.
// Twin of twin_a_accept.schway: the two members' CALLER body (escort) is
// byte-identical apart from this module header/name -- verified mechanically
// by TestInterproceduralTwinPairsDifferOnlyInTheCallee, which parses both
// members and asserts the caller's own ast.LinearBody (binding count, each
// binding's RHS.Kind/RHS.Source/RHS.Callee/RHS.Arguments, and Result) match
// exactly. The ONE thing that differs is escortee's DECLARED return type:
// here, `-> borrow(buffer) Buffer` (a shared borrow of escortee's own
// parameter), giving `core.FunctionSignature.Return.Mode == "shared"` and
// `returnsBorrowOfParam == true` in the interprocedural summary table.
//
// Expected: `check` refuses with check.interprocedural_loan_liveness. escort
// borrows buffer, calls escortee (which the summary table proves extends the
// loan across the call boundary onto `aliased`, D-08-07), then takes buffer
// while that interprocedurally-extended loan is still live (`aliased` is
// returned afterward) -- exactly the shape 08-01's relay_escort_witness.schway
// already proved for an EXCLUSIVE borrow; this fixture is the SHARED-borrow
// sibling, landed as a byte-comparable twin pair rather than a lone witness
// (T-08-09's mitigation: a caller-varying pair would pass with the
// interprocedural law deleted; this pair varies only the callee).
//
// This is the ONLY member of this plan's real .schway corpus that the plan's
// own <verify> block checks via the shipped CLI end to end (`go run ./cmd/schway
// --json check`) -- see 08-03-SUMMARY.md's Deviations for why the ACCEPT twin
// cannot make the same end-to-end claim (corevalidate's own, still
// intraprocedural loanChainIndex unconditionally treats an OpCall's target as
// inheriting every loan its SourceID carries, D-03-02's peer half, Phase 09
// scope).

module phase08.twin_a_refuse

export {
  fn escort
}

fn escortee(buffer: Buffer) -> borrow(buffer) Buffer {
  let observed = borrow buffer
  observed
}

fn escort(buffer: Buffer) -> borrow(buffer) Buffer {
  let borrowed = borrow buffer
  let aliased = escortee(borrowed)
  let delivered = take buffer
  aliased
}
